WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.914 --> 00:00:10.080
UYANGA TSEDEV: So hi, girls.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.420
My name is Uyanga.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.820
And I am currently
at the MIT Koch

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.154
Institute, which is a building
just for cancer research.

00:00:17.154 --> 00:00:18.570
And today, I'm
going to talk about

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.010
the biological engineering
that we do in order to find

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:26.910
and image deeply embedded and
difficult-to-reach tumors.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.990
So currently in the clinic,
the number one therapy method

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:36.840
for cancer is-- thank
you-- for cancer

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.390
is surgical debulking, which is
basically removing of the tumor

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.480
masses, and then chemotherapy.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.240
And so it's very
important for surgeons

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.390
to be able to locate
precisely and early

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.920
on in the stage of the
tumor the masses that they

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.030
want to remove in order to do
the most effective therapy.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.310
And so cancer
cells are basically

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:03.040
cells that have unusual
dividing abilities.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.430
So they proliferate
without stop.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:10.090
And they are differentiated from
the regular cells around them

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:13.770
by certain markers on
the surface of the cell.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:16.610
And so what we want to do is
use these markers on the surface

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:22.340
in the cell in order to
find and then locate where

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:23.964
the tumors are, and
tell the surgeons,

00:01:23.964 --> 00:01:25.380
this is the place
where you should

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.310
remove the biological tissue.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.730
And you don't want to damage
the healthy tissue around it.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.021
So you want to be very precise.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.600
So there are two tools
that we use in our lab

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.200
in order to find the
tumors in the body.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.120
The first is the
custom-built imager

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.710
that we're working on with
collaboration at the Lincoln

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.850
Labs, and the second is
the biological probe.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:56.750
It's a little shuttle
that we use in order

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.714
to go ahead and find
the cells in the body.

00:02:00.714 --> 00:02:02.380
So we can talk about
both of them today.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:06.950
So the biological
marker that we use

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.090
is called the M13 bacteriophage.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.300
And bacteriophage really
just means bacteria eater.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:13.620
So it's a virus.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:20.790
So as with typical viruses, it
infects bacteria, [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:24.240
takes over the local
mechanism of the bacteria,

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.200
and begins to replicate itself.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.680
So what we can do
is hack this system

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.410
in order to use this molecule
for our own benefits.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:36.050
It's a very beautiful, very
simple sort of molecule.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.250
It's just a piece
of DNA-- so a DNA

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.300
that codes for its
genetic information--

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.870
and a bunch of proteins that's
wrapped around the body.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.930
And so because we can
manipulate the DNA,

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.770
we can change the peptides that
are on the body of the phage.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.890
And so we can change
the protein so that they

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:55.780
have specific binding
abilities to certain materials

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.040
or certain ligands,
or certain markers.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.750
So this is the tumor cell
markers that I'm talking about.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:05.940
We can change the protein
at the head of the virus

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:10.290
in order to bind specifically
to the surface of a cancer cell.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.580
And we can change the
proteins on the body

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.680
of the virus to bind to
a specific imaging agent

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.900
or therapy agent that
we can then shuttle

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.611
to the surface of our cells.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:28.500
So for our system here, the
imaging agent that we chose

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.570
is called a carbon nanotube.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:34.900
And so a carbon nanotube
is just a piece of graphene

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.940
that's been rolled up.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.960
And what graphene
is is it's just

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.140
a single layer of graphite.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:44.370
And graphite is what you find
in your pencils, in lead.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.090
And so it has very interesting
optical and electrical

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:48.860
properties, and it's
very well-studied.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:50.370
But for us, what's
interesting about

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.460
it is the wavelength at
which it emits light.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:57.440
This is in the second window
near infrared wavelength.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.320
And so it's slightly longer
than the visible wavelength.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:05.670
And the cool thing about this
is it's able to penetrate deeper

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:06.927
into the biological tissue.

00:04:06.927 --> 00:04:09.010
And you have less scattering
and less interference

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:10.301
from the surrounding materials.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:20.220
So finally, this is the
design of our shuttles

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.920
that we send in to
find tumor cells.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.710
We have the M13
bacteriophage, which

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:28.640
has a particular
protein at the head that

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.160
detects the tumor cells.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.870
And we have on the body the
CNT, the carbon nanotube,

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:36.570
that is going to
light up and tell us

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.778
this is where we should go
ahead and remove the mass.

00:04:42.637 --> 00:04:44.970
So the first model that we
have actually done in our lab

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.080
is on ovarian tumor.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.930
And ovarian tumor is one
of the tumor [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:52.900
that is very difficult
to detect early on.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:55.040
Unlike things like breast
tumor, you can't feel it.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.930
And usually when the surgeon
or the doctor diagnoses,

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.110
it's quite late in the
stage of the tumor.

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:01.920
So it could have
turned metastatic.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.614
And metastatic means
that the tumor cells

00:05:04.614 --> 00:05:06.280
have detached from
the body and traveled

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:09.807
to other parts of the body, and
so proliferated other tumors

00:05:09.807 --> 00:05:10.640
throughout the body.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.950
So this is a stage you
don't want to reach.

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:18.900
So it'd be very cool if you
can put our probes into mice

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.850
with a ovarian tumor,
light up the tumors,

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:24.000
give the surgeon the
image, and the surgeon can

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.870
go ahead and remove the tumors,
and the mice will then survive.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.714
So this is a first step
in putting our system

00:05:29.714 --> 00:05:30.880
to its clinical application.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:36.180
So this is what
happens in real life.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:39.990
You see here the stomach
cavity of the mice.

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:43.470
And in the bright orange,
you see all the tumor masses

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:44.550
that have lit up.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.130
So we have injected
the mice with trillions

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:49.010
of our tiny little
molecular shuttles

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:52.480
that carry our CNT to the
location of the tumor.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:57.980
And using our imager, which
we have built with help

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:00.270
from the Lincoln
Labs, the surgeon

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:02.440
is able to go in and
remove all these nodules.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.980
And the cool thing
about the nodules

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.030
here is we're getting
the ones that are even

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:08.680
below a millimeter in diameter.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:11.720
So usually, when a surgeon
goes in without any guidance,

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.000
he might miss these because
he's just going by eye.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.700
But because he
has the imager, he

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:17.741
can go ahead and remove
most of the tumors.

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:24.440
So here's an example of what
we're doing in real time.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:28.680
So during surgery, if
the surgeon can directly

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.600
refer to the images
that he's getting,

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:34.400
then he can go ahead and
implement our technology

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:36.040
in a very real way.

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:39.425
So here, we see Dr.
[INAUDIBLE] from MGH.

00:06:39.425 --> 00:06:41.280
He's doing surgery on mice.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:45.107
And you can see on the screen,
he can see brightly lit up--

00:06:45.107 --> 00:06:46.690
this is where the
tumors are, and this

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:47.830
is where I should take it out.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:49.980
And so here's a serial
image of what's happening.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:54.710
So here's the probes lighting
up where the tumors are.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:57.550
This is what happens when
the surgeon goes in by eye

00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:59.550
and is like, OK,
from my experience,

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:01.410
this is where I should
take out the tumors.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:04.050
And you see that there's
quite a bit of mass left.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.520
But if we have the
imager, then we

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.730
have a much cleaner image
at the end of the surgery.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:10.626
And so there's a
lot less of a chance

00:07:10.626 --> 00:07:12.000
that the tumor is
going to recur.

00:07:16.539 --> 00:07:19.080
So for me personally, there are
two things I'm doing in order

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.620
to expand on our system
that we have already built.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.650
The first is manipulating
the M13 phage

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:28.390
to different geometries.

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:29.590
So I call this inhophage.

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:33.860
Inho just means
small in Portuguese.

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.760
And so based on the aspect
ratio, which basically

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:39.630
is the diameter versus
the length of our phage,

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.410
they might have very
different traveling abilities

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:43.450
in the bloodstream.

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:44.980
So because of
their oblong shape,

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:48.760
they have a tendency to stick
to the walls and tumble around.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:51.781
But if we can change this
shape to an optimal size,

00:07:51.781 --> 00:07:53.280
then there's a
chance that they will

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.070
travel for a longer period
of time in the bloodstream,

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.060
and so find the tumors
that are farther

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.950
from the site of injection.

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:03.690
The second thing is we are
expanding on the library

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:07.080
of cancers that we can attack.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.420
So so far, we have
looked at ovarian tumor,

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.664
and now we want to
look at brain tumor.

00:08:11.664 --> 00:08:13.580
And the most difficult
thing about brain tumor

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:15.290
is the blood-brain barrier.

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:17.700
So it's just a layer of cells
that separate your blood

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.020
from the matter in your brain.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:21.610
And the molecules
that pass through

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:23.480
are very specific
and very small.

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:26.980
So it's very hard to deliver
from your bloodstream

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:32.250
various drugs or imaging agents
to the actual brain mass.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:35.299
So what we have done
here is actually

00:08:35.299 --> 00:08:37.700
engineer the head
peptides-- so the proteins

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:42.440
at the head of our phage--
so that it allows for travel

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:46.630
across the blood-brain barrier
so it can deliver these imaging

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:48.810
molecules to tumors
in the brain.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:56.700
So one thing to remember here
is that the scale at which we're

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:58.680
working is very, very small.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:01.650
So if you look at
CNT or our phage,

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:05.986
really, it's about
100,000 times smaller

00:09:05.986 --> 00:09:07.110
than a strand of your hair.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:10.110
So these are really,
really tiny things.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:13.040
So when I make
these small phage,

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:15.010
they're even smaller
than our regular phage.

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:18.500
Our regular phage is
about 800 nanometers.

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:22.530
So here's an example of 280
nanometer phage, 100 nanometer

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:24.782
phage, and 50 nanometer phage.

00:09:24.782 --> 00:09:26.740
So when I make them and
I want to look at them,

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:28.580
it's really hard because
they're so small.

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:31.440
And you can't just use
regular light microscopy

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.090
to detect them.

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:34.740
So another interesting
thing that I do

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.152
is use atomic force microscopy.

00:09:37.152 --> 00:09:38.610
I don't know if
you've heard of it.

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:42.330
But what it is is basically
you take a sample,

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:44.360
and you have this
very, very tiny needle

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:46.780
that scans across the surface.

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:49.450
And so you're getting a
topology of the surface.

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:52.460
And from that scanning, kind
of like reading Braille,

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:55.640
you get a good idea of what
the profile of your image is.

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:58.101
So these are the images that
I get from this sort of needle

00:09:58.101 --> 00:09:58.600
probing.

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:06.740
For the brain tumor,
another interesting factor--

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:10.620
not only can we bring
the phage to the surface

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:13.592
of the tumor-- so this is
tumor here shown in green.

00:10:13.592 --> 00:10:16.050
This is tumor that actually
has green fluorescent proteins,

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:18.600
so that means they glow.

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:21.170
And if we send in our
phage, we see that, oh,

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:26.430
yeah, our phage goes ahead
and coats in red the tumor.

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:27.980
But then the other
thing we notice

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:31.381
is that the phage is actually
internalized into the tumor

00:10:31.381 --> 00:10:31.880
cells.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:35.050
So the phage comes and
attaches to the surface,

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:37.400
and then the tumor
cells eats the phage.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:41.840
So in the blue here is
the nucleus of a phage.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:45.700
And in the red spread around
the nucleus in the Golgi region

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:46.970
is our phage.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:50.480
So it just tells us we can
possibly deliver things to

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:53.150
inside the cells of the cancer.

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:55.260
So that means we could
send in therapies

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:58.360
that disrupt the function,
and so in this way,

00:10:58.360 --> 00:10:59.830
kill the tumor cells.

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:02.220
And so can we not only
detect where they are

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.060
and see if it can
remove them effectively,

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:07.100
but we can also
locally cure them.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:17.340
So overall, today, we've looked
at the very real medical impact

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:20.050
that we can do with
imaging and the probe

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:22.080
that we have biologically
engineered in our lab.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.440
And the two things
that I've done

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:27.844
is look at what we can do
to change the geometry,

00:11:27.844 --> 00:11:30.260
and what we can do to change
the different cancers that we

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:30.810
can attack.

00:11:34.742 --> 00:11:36.200
And all of this
work, of course, is

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:38.930
impossible without
the collaboration that

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:43.190
happens between the colleagues I
have at the MIT Koch Institute,

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:46.142
as well as the scientists
we have at the Lincoln Labs.

00:11:46.142 --> 00:11:48.600
And so that's another really
cool thing about this project.

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:50.840
It's so very broad,
and so it brings

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:52.590
in so many different
techniques and brings

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:53.770
in so many different people.

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:55.750
And so I really
want to thank them.

00:11:55.750 --> 00:12:00.320
And another aspect of this work
that I really find meaningful

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:04.000
is that I am personally
funded by families

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.180
that are affected by
these types of tumors,

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:07.250
these types of diseases.

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:12.140
And so I really want to thank
the Goodwin, [INAUDIBLE],

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:15.830
[INAUDIBLE] and [INAUDIBLE]
families who have been really

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:17.940
generous with their
funding for this project.

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:24.959
And finally, I want to say
that for me, the M13 is

00:12:24.959 --> 00:12:26.000
kind of a building block.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:28.330
We're here all as
Girls Who Build.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:32.190
And so for me, for my building
project, it's the M13.

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:36.970
And I hope that you guys are
able to understand from this

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:39.110
that as an engineer,
for me, it's really just

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:43.670
about finding creative ways to
use my building blocks to make

00:12:43.670 --> 00:12:46.270
a difference, to address
the problems that I think

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:49.150
are most important to me.

00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:52.580
And so thank you very
much for listening.

00:12:52.580 --> 00:12:55.780
Please feel free to
ask me any questions.

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:59.480
I can talk more about myself, or
I can talk more about research.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:01.760
But yes, thank you.

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:05.260
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:10.443
PROFESSOR: Any
questions for her?

00:13:10.443 --> 00:13:11.234
UYANGA TSEDEV: Yes?

00:13:11.234 --> 00:13:13.604
AUDIENCE: You were
using specific examples

00:13:13.604 --> 00:13:18.520
for the cancer, but can these
detect any type of cancer,

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:21.852
or is it just specific,
or a couple of types?

00:13:21.852 --> 00:13:23.810
UYANGA TSEDEV: It can
detect any type of cancer

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:28.830
as long as you have a specific
differentiating marker

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:31.460
on the cancer that you know of.

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:34.320
So not only is this
useful in cancer,

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:37.320
it's also useful in
detecting any other material.

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:39.460
As long as you're able
to modify the head so

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:44.700
that it is specific to that
material or to that cell,

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:48.160
then you can do that, which
makes it a very elegant, very

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.650
cool molecule, which is
why I think it's something

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:51.630
I should be using.

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:52.830
